Welcome to notebook fifteen. This is the bridge. Everything in notebooks one through fourteen was the foundation. This notebook shows where each piece lands once you open a Spark codebase. The reason Spark is a Scala library, not just a java virtual machine library, is that its API surface leans hard on language features you have now seen. Case classes for schemas. Pattern matching for row shapes. Traits and generics for the type safe Dataset API. Implicits, now given and using in Scala three, for everything from encoders to syntactic extensions. And lambdas for the transformations themselves.

You will not become a Spark expert in one notebook. The companion apache spark track covers R D Ds, data frames, the execution model, joins, tuning, structured streaming, and Delta Lake in depth. The goal here is narrower. When you open a Spark file and see Dataset of Row, import spark dot implicits, group by key, or a u d f taking a string lambda, every line should look like Scala you already know, not like a separate dialect. By the end you should be able to read idiomatic Scala Spark code without surprise, and recognise which Spark constructs map back to which Scala features from earlier in this track.

Spark exposes three abstractions for distributed data. All three are ordinary Scala types. You reason about them with the intuitions you have already built. First, R D D of A. A distributed immutable collection of A. The lowest level API. Closest in feel to List of A, except partitioned across machines. Second, data frame. A distributed table with named typed columns. Internally a Dataset of Row. The most common type in modern code. Third, Dataset of A. A distributed collection of A where A is typically a case class. Combines the columnar engine of data frame with the type safety of R D D. The Scala first API.

The most useful single fact to memorise. Data frame is Dataset of Row. It is a type alias. Read that twice. It explains why a lot of Spark documentation moves between the two names without warning.

So in code, you create a spark session with spark session dot builder dot app name notebook fifteen dot master local star dot get or create. Then val d f equals spark dot read dot j son of a path. The type ascription data frame for d f, or Dataset of Row, both compile, because they are the same type.

Now to the first crossover. Case classes are schemas. A case class is the natural way to give a Spark row a name and a type. Spark inspects the case class at compile time, derives an Encoder, and uses it to do the binary serialisation that lets the columnar engine run at speed. The case class fields become column names. The field types become column types. Everything you learned about case classes in notebook seven, copy, equality by value, pattern matching, and destructuring, still applies. The case class is not a separate Spark concept. It is the same Scala concept, with one extra superpower. Spark can derive a schema from it.

So you define case class Order taking id a long, user id a long, amount a big decimal, and currency a string. Import spark dot implicits dot star, which brings encoders for case classes into scope. Now val orders equals spark dot read dot parquet of orders, dot as of Order, gives you a Dataset of Order. Every transformation is type checked end to end. Filter on order amount greater than one hundred, returns a Dataset of Order. Map on order id, returns a Dataset of long.

The import spark dot implicits line is what notebook eleven called a using import. It brings into scope the encoders the compiler needs to type check as of Order, the map call, and the dollar column syntax. Without it, the compiler cannot prove the type can be serialised, and you get a noisy error message. The fix is always, import the implicits.

Now pattern matching on rows. When you are working with untyped data frames, each record is a Row. A sequence of values keyed by column name or position. Extracting fields by hand is verbose. Pattern matching, with the same Row extractor shape you saw in notebook eight, makes it short. So you can write events dot map of a partial function with three cases. Case Row of id a long, then the literal click, then url a string, arrow the string click id arrow url. Case Row of id, then view, then anything, arrow the string view id. Case Row of id, kind a string, anything, arrow the string other kind id. The match is the same match you used on Option and Either. Spark just provides the Row extractor.

If you find yourself doing this a lot, that is the signal to move from Dataset of Row to Dataset of A for a case class A. Then field access is dot user id instead of case Row of id a long, underscore, underscore. And the compiler tells you when the schema drifts.

Now to familiar combinators with a different runtime. Most of the Dataset API will look familiar from notebooks four and five on collections. Map, flat map, filter, group by, reduce, aggregate. Same names. Same intent. The difference is where they run. A list dot map runs on this thread, now. A Dataset dot map runs on whatever executor holds each partition, eventually, when an action like count, collect, or write forces it. The shapes you write are nearly identical. The laziness is much deeper. A Dataset does not materialise until you ask for a result.

So big equals orders dot filter on amount greater than one hundred is just a description. By currency equals orders dot group by currency dot count is also just a description. Nothing has been computed. Calling big dot write dot parquet, or by currency dot show, is what makes Spark run the plan.

Now to Option meeting nullability. Spark columns are nullable by default. Inside a Dataset of A, the natural way to model, this field might be null, is Option of T on the case class. The encoder handles the translation. None becomes a sequel null. Some of v becomes v. So case class User takes id a long, name a string, and email an Option of String. The Dataset of User reads parquet and ascribes as of User. Now you handle absence the way you have handled it everywhere else. Filter on email dot is defined. Or flat map on email mapping the at sign split, to get a Dataset of String of email domains.

Reaching for row dot get as of string of email on a raw Row is the brittle alternative. It returns null for missing fields, which propagates straight into a null pointer exception downstream. The exact failure mode Option was designed to remove. Stay in Dataset of A whenever you can.

Now to Either for per row error handling. A common need is per record validation, where some rows pass and some fail, and you want both. The clean stream to write downstream, and the failed stream to send to a dead letter location. The shape from notebook nine maps directly. A pure Scala function returns Either of Bad and Clean. Two flat map passes split the source into typed good and bad outputs.

So define case classes for Raw, Clean, and Bad. The parse function takes a Raw, returns Either of Bad and Clean. The body is Try of big decimal of the amount string, dot to either, dot left dot map building a Bad with the error message, dot map building a Clean with the parsed amount. Now val raw is the Dataset of Raw. Val good is raw dot flat map taking r to parse r dot to option. Val bad is raw dot flat map taking r to parse r dot left dot to option. Each flat map produces a typed Dataset of the appropriate case class.

Two passes over raw. Two outputs. One source. The Either is the contract between the validation step and the split. Exactly the role it played in notebook nine, scaled out across partitions. If re parsing twice would be expensive in your case, raw dot cache before the two flat maps is the usual fix.

Now a small note on implicits in Spark. The reason you see import spark dot implicits dot star at the top of nearly every Scala Spark file is that the Dataset API depends on type class style instances for two things. Encoder of A. How to serialise A. And a handful of column syntax helpers, like the dollar column notation. In Scala two vocabulary these were implicit vals. In the Scala three vocabulary you have been using, they are givens with using imports. The Spark codebase itself is mid migration in many places. You will see both implicit and given in real code. The semantics are the same. The rules from notebook eleven apply unchanged.

Now to u d fs, the optimizer's blind spot. When the built in column operations, like when, concat, regexp extract, and the sequel functions, do not express what you need, you reach for a user defined function. U d fs let you embed arbitrary Scala into the query. But the Catalyst optimizer cannot see inside them. To Spark, a u d f is an opaque box. That has two consequences worth knowing before you write one. First, the optimizer cannot push filters or projections through a u d f, so a column you only needed to feed the u d f gets read in full. Second, the per row Scala to internal format conversion adds overhead the typed Dataset of A API does not pay. Prefer the typed Dataset dot map for one off logic. And prefer the built in sequel functions whenever they exist. Reach for a u d f only when neither covers the case.

So in code, you define val upper equals u d f of a lambda string to string to upper case. Then with column name underscore upper, calling upper on column name, gives a data frame with the transformed column. The typed alternative on a Dataset of User is users dot map of u to u dot copy with name dot to upper case. The map version is visible to the compiler. Much faster for case class shaped data.

Now to laziness at the Spark scale. You met laziness in two earlier places. Lazy val defers a single value's computation until the first read. Collection views defer a chain of transformations until you force a result. Dataset is the third place. And the most important. Every map, filter, or select you write is recorded into a logical plan. Nothing runs until an action like count, collect, show, or write triggers it.

That recording is what makes whole pipeline optimisation possible. Catalyst sees the entire chain, rewrites it. Predicate pushdown. Projection pruning. Join reordering. Then it generates code. Calling actions in the middle of a pipeline, for debug counts especially, forces re execution from the top and is a common cause of slow jobs.

Now to futures and Spark. The Spark driver coordinates work, but each action on a Dataset blocks the calling thread until the job completes. You can wrap actions in Future to run multiple Spark queries concurrently against the same spark session. Useful for unrelated reads or writes that share cluster capacity. The pattern is unsurprising given notebook thirteen. Val a equals Future of spark dot read dot parquet of orders dot count. Val b equals Future of spark dot read dot parquet of users dot count. Then Await dot result of a dot zip b, with a one minute timeout, gives you a tuple of the two counts.

Both jobs are submitted to the same Spark scheduler and run in parallel against the cluster's resources. Do not use Future to parallelise transformations within a single Dataset. Spark already parallelises those across executors. Use it only when you want to run multiple independent Spark jobs at once.

Now to the spark session as a resource. A spark session is a heavyweight resource. It holds connections to the cluster, caches metadata, and consumes driver memory. In tests, scripts, or short lived jobs, give it a single owner and a clear shutdown. The patterns from notebook fourteen apply directly. So you define a given Using dot Releasable of spark session with, def release taking a session s equals s dot stop. Then wrap Using on spark session dot builder dot app name ad hoc dot get or create, with a body that does its work. The session is stopped on exit, even on failure.

In a long running service the lifetime is different. A single session for the life of the process. Shut down on graceful termination. But in any one shot context, the Using pattern keeps you from leaking the driver process. This is the same reasoning that made Using worth learning in notebook fourteen, applied at a different scale.

A short list of pitfalls specific to the Scala meets Spark boundary, on top of the ones in notebook fourteen. First, forgetting import spark dot implicits dot star. The error is usually no encoder for some type, or value dollar is not a member of string context. The fix is always the import. Second, capturing the driver in a closure. A Dataset dot map lambda is serialised and shipped to executors. If it captures a non serialisable value from the surrounding scope, like a spark session, a database connection, or a logger, the task fails at runtime with a task not serializable error. Refactor to construct the value inside the lambda, or use map partitions so the construction cost is paid once per partition. Third, calling actions in a loop. Each action triggers a full re execution of the plan. Dataset dot cache is the lever when you really do need the same result twice. Fourth, using u d fs when built ins or typed map would do. Worth repeating, because it is the single most common performance smell in Scala Spark code. Fifth, confusing Dataset of A with R D D of A. They have similar looking APIs, but Dataset goes through Catalyst and R D D does not. New code should be Dataset, or data frame, unless you have a specific reason. Sixth, mutable state in transformations. A var inside a map lambda is meaningless across partitions. Each executor sees its own copy. Use aggregate, group by key, or accumulators instead.

Let us close with a small but realistic pipeline that ties everything together. Read a j son file of orders, parse them into a case class, route invalid rows to a side output, aggregate the rest by user, and write both results. Every construct here came from an earlier notebook.

Define case classes RawOrder, Order, BadOrder, and UserTotal. Define parse taking a RawOrder and returning Either of BadOrder and Order. The body is Try of big decimal of the amount string, to either, left dot map to BadOrder with the error message, and map to Order with the parsed amount, the user id, and the currency. Provide a given Using dot Releasable for spark session so the session shuts down cleanly. Now Using on the spark session builder, with a body. Inside, import spark dot implicits dot star. Val raw equals the j son read as of RawOrder, dot cache so two passes do not re read.

Val good is raw dot flat map of r to parse r dot to option, giving a Dataset of Order. Val bad is raw dot flat map of r to parse r dot left dot to option, giving a Dataset of BadOrder. Val totals is good dot group by key on user id, dot reduce groups by adding amounts via the copy method, dot map of a partial function on uid and o, yielding UserTotal of uid and the summed amount. Then totals dot write dot mode overwrite dot parquet totals. And bad dot write dot mode overwrite dot parquet bad orders.

Read that top to bottom. Case classes from notebook seven model the schemas. Try dot to either from notebooks nine and fourteen captures parse failures. Either routes good and bad to separate outputs. Group by key and reduce groups from notebook five's collection idioms scale to billions of records without changing shape. The Using block from notebook fourteen owns the spark session lifetime.

There is no separate Spark language you needed to learn. There is Scala, and there are Spark types that participate in Scala's standard combinators. That is the whole point of the track. Every feature notebooks one through fourteen covered has a payoff in this last picture.

This is the end of the Scala foundation. From here, the work splits in two directions, and you can take either or both. Depth in Spark. The companion apache spark track picks up exactly where this notebook ended. Data frames in detail. The execution model. Joins and partitioning. Spark sequel and u d fs. Performance tuning. Structured streaming. Delta Lake. Open the first apache spark notebook to start. Or depth in Scala. Cats, cats effect, Z I O, f s two. The ecosystem around pure functional programming and effect systems. None of it is required for productive Spark work. But learning at least one effect system is the single largest jump in general Scala fluency available after this track.

You have the whole language now. Go build something with it.
